          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Legacy in Communication</strong><br>\nBell's influence extended beyond his inventions as he helped establish the National Geographic Society and served as its second president, promoting the dissemination of geographic knowledge.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Views on Aviation</strong><br>\nBell also contributed to the field of aeronautics by founding the Aerial Experiment Association in 1907, which aimed to build powered aircraft and develop flying machines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Lasting Impact</strong><br>\nBell's death in 1922 marked the end of an era for great inventors. His legacy, especially the telephone, fundamentally changed global communications and laid the groundwork for the telecommunications industry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
